-- title:
--   A single composable RAM block performs Euclidean long division.
-- statement:
--   A single composable RAM block performs Euclidean long division. The caller supplies
--   a numerator in data register 0, a positive divisor in 1, and a bit bound k in index 0.
--   It returns the remainder in 0 and quotient in 3. Only scalar registers 0 through 5
--   and address register 1 are modified.
--
--   This lemma is part of OpenAI's machine-checked proof that rational linear programs can be solved by a strongly polynomial algorithm on a real-number RAM (Smale's ninth problem). It is used in the proof of `OAI.Optimization.LinearProgramming.Compression.reducedScalarPairBlock_spec`, `OAI.Optimization.LinearProgramming.Compression.serializeNaturalBlock_spec`.
--
